  "scope": "This mitigation covers ONLY the two copies of picomatch that pnpm installs and audits: picomatch@4.0.7 on its 74 recorded paths (six of them, through @sentry/nextjs, in the production dependency tree) and picomatch@2.3.2 on its one development path. Both are patched with upstream picomatch pull request #246 at f03bec2c, an UNMERGED proposal by an outside contributor, not a maintainer-accepted fix. It targets the 2.x line; the 4.0.7 patch applies the same three lib/parse.js hunks at the equivalent points. picomatch's maintainer has publicly disputed the advisory (globs are regular expressions; do not let untrusted users supply them), so an official fix may never be published. The patch removes the exponential backtracking for the advisory's named patterns, +(*) and +(*a), and for any repeated extglob whose single branch holds only literal characters and single stars (+(a*b), *(*.js) and so on), without changing what they match; in capture mode those patterns are matched literally instead. It does NOT cover other shapes, which stayed exponential on the patched copies when measured for #5252: a branch holding ?, a bracket class, a quote or an escape (for example +(*[ab])), and a body of several branches with a star (for example +(*a|b)). Nor does it cover the copies compiled into other packages, listed below, which pnpm can neither patch nor audit. Exposure is limited because the pattern itself must come from an attacker: no code under src/, scripts/, e2e/ or prisma/ imports a glob library, every pattern the tools use comes from this repository's configuration or the tools' own defaults, and at run time Next.js matches image URLs only against image patterns fixed in its configuration: none are configured here, so it applies its own default local pattern `**` and no remote pattern; a visitor supplies only the URL being matched, never a pattern.",
  "knownUncoveredCopies": [
